          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="companion-card" data-type="cat" data-days="25">
          <div class="card-image-wrapper">
            <span class="status-tag">25 days waiting</span>
            <img class="card-photo" src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1503777119540-ce54b422baff?auto=format&fit=crop&w=500&h=500&q=80" alt="Calico cat, stand-in photo for Bella's simulated profile" loading="lazy">
            <span class="photo-credit">Uriel Soberanes / Unsplash</span>
          </div>
          <div class="card-content">
            <div class="card-header"><h3 class="pet-name">Bella</h3><span class="pet-breed">Longhair Calico · 2 yrs</span></div>
            <div class="card-location">Simulated shelter cohort</div>
            <button class="view-details-btn" data-pet="Bella">View match requirements →</button>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="demo">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div class="demo-intro" style="max-width:none;">
          <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.7rem,3vw,2.3rem);">Run the cohort yourself.</h2>
          <p style="margin-top:8px;">This runs live, in your browser, over today's six-animal cohort. Answer the same question a coordinator would ask an applicant, and Kyndra will filter, derive, and settle a stable assignment for the whole cohort — including yours.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="demo-layout">
        <div class="intake-card">
          <h4>Applicant intake</h4>
          <p class="hint">Six households have already applied. Fill this in and Kyndra adds you to the pool.</p>

          <div class="field">
            <label>Is there a specific animal you're here for?</label>
            <div class="pill-group" id="specificGroup">
              <button class="pill selected" data-value="">No — match me on fit</button>
              <button class="pill" data-value="bruno">Yes — Bruno</button>
              <button class="pill" data-value="luna">Yes — Luna</button>
              <button class="pill" data-value="perro">Yes — Perro</button>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="field">
            <label>Home type</label>
            <div class="pill-group" id="homeGroup">
              <button class="pill" data-value="yard">House with a yard</button>
              <button class="pill selected" data-value="apartment">Apartment, no yard</button>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="field">
            <label>Young children at home</label>
            <div class="pill-group" id="kidsGroup">
              <button class="pill selected" data-value="no">No</button>
              <button class="pill" data-value="yes">Yes</button>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="field">
            <label>Other pets already in the home</label>
            <div class="pill-group" id="petsGroup">
              <button class="pill selected" data-value="">None</button>
              <button class="pill" data-value="dog">A dog</button>
              <button class="pill" data-value="cat">A cat</button>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="field">
            <label>Experience with this kind of animal</label>
            <div class="pill-group" id="expGroup">
              <button class="pill selected" data-value="first-time">First-time owner</button>
              <button class="pill" data-value="experienced">Experienced</button>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="field">
            <label>Energy level you're looking for</label>
            <div class="pill-group" id="energyGroup">
              <button class="pill" data-value="low">Calm</button>
              <button class="pill selected" data-value="medium">Moderate</button>
              <button class="pill" data-value="high">High-energy</button>
            </div>
          </div>

          <button class="run-btn" id="runMatch">Run the matching engine →</button>
        </div>

        <div class="results-panel">
          <div class="results-empty" id="resultsEmpty">
            Fill in the intake and run the engine to see where the whole cohort lands — not just you.
          </div>
          <div class="results-board" id="resultsBoard">
            <div class="board-summary">
              <div class="board-stat"><b class="tnum" id="statMatched">0</b><span>Matched</span></div>
              <div class="board-stat"><b class="tnum" id="statUnmatched">0</b><span>Unmatched</span></div>
              <div class="board-stat"><b class="tnum" id="statChecks">0</b><span>Checks run</span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="match-list" id="matchList"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-grid" id="postMatchTools" style="display:none">
        <div class="tool-card">
          <h4>Attempt a swap</h4>
          <p class="hint">Pick two matched animals. Kyndra checks whether swapping their applicants would leave both sides better off — a blocking pair. If it can't find one, the match is stable.</p>
          <select id="swapA"></select>
          <select id="swapB"></select>
          <button class="swap-btn" id="runSwap">Attempt the swap</button>
          <div class="swap-result" id="swapResult"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="tool-card">
          <h4>Long-stay equity dial</h4>
          <p class="hint">Ties only. It can shift which animal wins a tied preference — it can never place an animal into a household that fails a hard constraint.</p>
          <div class="dial-row">
            <input type="range" id="equityDial" min="0" max="10" value="0">
            <span class="dial-value tnum" id="dialValue">0</span>
          </div>
          <div class="dial-note">0 = no equity weighting. 10 = ties always favor the longest-waiting animal.</div>
          <div class="dial-outcome" id="dialOutcome">Run the match first, then move the dial to see who it affects.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="tool-card">
          <h4>Unmatched analysis</h4>
          <p class="hint">Animals nobody in the current applicant pool could take, and why — read as a recruitment target, not a failure.</p>
          <div class="unmatched-list" id="unmatchedList"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="tool-card">
          <h4>Impact, in two numbers</h4>
          <p class="hint">Kept separate on purpose — one is provable from this run, one is a labeled projection.</p>
          <div class="impact-row">
            <div class="impact-box"><b class="tnum" id="impactChecks">0</b><span>pairwise judgements run instantly, provable</span></div>
            <div class="impact-box"><b class="tnum" id="impactWelfare">—</b><span>projected mismatches avoided this run</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="impact-toggle">
            <button class="active" data-mode="conservative" id="btnConservative">Conservative</button>
            <button data-mode="optimistic" id="btnOptimistic">Optimistic</button>
          </div>
          <p class="impact-caveat">Directionally indicative, not a causal estimate. Kyndra's constraints don't capture the full behavioural variance Powell et al. measured — see citations below.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <button class="stability-btn" id="verifyStability" style="margin-top:18px">Verify: no blocking pairs exist</button>
      <div class="swap-result" id="stabilityResult" style="margin-top:12px"></div>

      <div id="evidence" class="citation-panel">
        <h4>What's real, what's simulated</h4>
        <div class="citation-item"><b>The cohort is simulated.</b> Six animals, sized consistent with Shelter Animals Count's published per-organisation averages — not sourced from any individual shelter's records, and not depicting real animals.</div>
        <div class="citation-item"><b>The constraints are real.</b> Powell et al. (2021, <i>Scientific Reports</i>), analysing 23,932 adoption records, found a 16.3% return rate for adopted dogs — 35% behavioural incompatibility, 18% conflict with an existing household pet. Every hard constraint above maps to one of those two documented causes.</div>
        <div class="citation-item"><b>The compounding cost is real.</b> Powell et al. (2022) followed returning adopters: only about 1 in 10 ever adopted again, and behavioural returners were four times less likely to than medical returners.</div>
        <div class="citation-item"><b>Why stability, not pure optimisation.</b> Wu et al. (2025, <i>JAMA Netw Open</i>) found a mixed-integer optimiser beats Gale–Shapley on ophthalmology-residency data — 78.4% vs 70.9% matched to a top-3 choice. That's a real trade-off: optimisation can raise average fit, but Gale–Shapley guarantees no shelter–applicant pair would rather defect. For a placement a household might return, we chose the guarantee.</div>
        <div class="honesty-note"><b>Kyndra proposes; it doesn't decide.</b> Framed as odds, not outcomes: this reduces the odds staff overlook a bad pairing under time pressure, not "prevents returns." Human-baseline and 500-cohort randomized comparisons are still running ahead of Sept 13 — see the PRD for status.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="how">
      <div class="section-head" style="margin-bottom:0; border-bottom:0; padding-bottom:0;">
        <div><h2>Matching, without the black box.</h2></div>
      </div>
      <div class="explainer">
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem; visibility:hidden; height:0; margin:0;">.</h3>
          <p style="margin-top:0; max-width:44ch; color:var(--ink-soft);">Kyndra does not act as an AI decision-maker. It proposes a stable assignment with reasons. Staff remain in control, and hard constraints are treated as actual constraints, not just lower scores.</p>
          <div class="reason">
            <b>Why this matters</b>
            A recommendation can be argued with. An opaque ranking can't.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="steps">
          <div class="step"><b>01</b><div><strong>Filter impossible pairs</strong><br><span>Constraint violations are eliminated, not down-ranked.</span></div></div>
          <div class="step"><b>02</b><div><strong>Derive both sides</strong><br><span>Applicant wants ≠ animal needs. Nobody hand-ranks a list.</span></div></div>
          <div class="step"><b>03</b><div><strong>Find a stable match</strong><br><span>Gale–Shapley deferred acceptance settles the whole cohort at once.</span></div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer" id="shelter">
    <div class="footer-content">
      <div class="footer-brand">
        <div class="nav-logo"><svg class="logo-mark" viewBox="0 0 32 32"><use href="#paw-icon-light"/></svg>Kyndra</div>
        <p>Transparent, explainable allocation for animal shelters. The system proposes; shelter staff decide.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="footer-links">
        <div class="link-column">
